The probability of rolling a 1 exactly six times when rolling six 6-sided dice is given by $P(1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1) = \left(\frac{1}{6}\right)^6 = \frac{1}{46656}$.

The probability of rolling a 2 exactly six times when rolling six 6-sided dice is given by $P(2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2) = \left(\frac{1}{6}\right)^6 = \frac{1}{46656}$.

We want to find the probability of rolling a 1 exactly six times and rolling a 2 exactly six times when rolling six 6-sided dice. This is the sum of the probabilities of these two events, since they are mutually exclusive.

$P(\text{six 1's and six 2's}) = P(\text{six 1's}) + P(\text{six 2's}) = \frac{1}{46656} + \frac{1}{46656} = \frac{2}{46656} = \frac{1}{23328}$.

