Here's how we can solve the problem step by step:

We're asked to compute the matrix product $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 4 & 8 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}$.
This is done by multiplying the rows of the first matrix by the columns of the second matrix.

The first row of the first matrix is $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \end{pmatrix}$, and the first column of the second matrix is $\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}$.  So the first element of the resulting matrix is $(1)(5) + (2)(4) = 5 + 8 = 13$.

The first row of the first matrix is $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \end{pmatrix}$, and the second column of the second matrix is $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}$.  So the second element of the resulting matrix is $(1)(3) + (2)(3) = 3 + 6 = 9$.

The second row of the first matrix is $\begin{pmatrix} 4 & 8 \end{pmatrix}$, and the first column of the second matrix is $\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}$.  So the third element of the resulting matrix is $(4)(5) + (8)(4) = 20 + 32 = 52$.

The second row of the first matrix is $\begin{pmatrix} 4 & 8 \end{pmatrix}$, and the second column of the second matrix is $\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}$.  So the fourth element of the resulting matrix is $(4)(3) + (8)(3) = 12 + 24 = 36$.

The result is the matrix $\begin{pmatrix} 13 & 9 \\ 52 & 36 \end{pmatrix}$.

