\begin{enumerate}
    \item We are given two linear equations: 
    $$x + y = 7$$
    $$x - y = 9$$
    \item We can solve this system of equations by adding the two equations together. Adding the two equations, we get:
    $$(x + y) + (x - y) = 7 + 9$$
    $$2x = 16$$
    $$x = \frac{16}{2}$$
    $$x = 8$$
    \item Now that we have the value of $x$, we can substitute it into either equation to solve for $y$. Let's use the first equation:
    $$x + y = 7$$
    $$8 + y = 7$$
    $$y = 7 - 8$$
    $$y = -1$$
    \item So we have $x = 8$ and $y = -1$. The answer is $x = 8$ and $y = -1$.
\end{enumerate}
