Let $a$ and $b$ be positive integers, neither of which is a multiple of 3.
We want to find the minimum possible remainder when $a^2+b^2$ is divided by 3.
Since $a$ and $b$ are not multiples of 3, we have $a \equiv 1$ or $2 \pmod{3}$ and $b \equiv 1$ or $2 \pmod{3}$.
We consider the four cases:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $a \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$ and $b \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$. Then $a^2 \equiv 1^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$ and $b^2 \equiv 1^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$. So, $a^2+b^2 \equiv 1+1 \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$.
    \item $a \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$ and $b \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$. Then $a^2 \equiv 1^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$ and $b^2 \equiv 2^2 \equiv 4 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$. So, $a^2+b^2 \equiv 1+1 \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$.
    \item $a \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$ and $b \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$. Then $a^2 \equiv 2^2 \equiv 4 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$ and $b^2 \equiv 1^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$. So, $a^2+b^2 \equiv 1+1 \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$.
    \item $a \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$ and $b \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$. Then $a^2 \equiv 2^2 \equiv 4 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$ and $b^2 \equiv 2^2 \equiv 4 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$. So, $a^2+b^2 \equiv 1+1 \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$.
\end{enumerate}
In all cases, $a^2+b^2 \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$. Thus the minimum possible remainder when $a^2+b^2$ is divided by 3 is 2.

